<b>ז.</b> “They come voluntarily”—unlike chatat, which comes only if one transgressed something requiring chatat; similarly asham—even though we find asham taluy and nazir who brings asham—still a plain asham does not come voluntarily. I saw in Maharshal one may say yehi ratzon after chatat and asham only in doubtful wording—“if I transgressed…” In my humble opinion this is wrong—we find olah brought as safek throughout Gemara (e.g., Kidushin)—but not chatat, since chatat is never voluntary—how could one say “if not liable it should be nedavah”? Nedavah is unrelated to chatat procedure—likewise here. I saw a sharp responsum: some say before parashat tamid and Eizehu mekomam “may the words of our lips be acceptable”—incorrect to protest them, but it implies everything following—including chatat laws—and “acceptable” does not fit voluntary korbanot lest one not be liable—like bringing hullin to the azarah—unless one says “may our lips substitute for tamid service” as explained.
